1
00:00:23,970 --> 00:02:13,680
 Jamais fléchis, non jamais

2
00:02:56,760 --> 00:02:59,970
 an experienced navigator, disposed to leading any such expedition.

3
00:03:00,450 --> 00:03:02,010
 A new spice route.

4
00:03:02,910 --> 00:03:05,810
 Yes, sire, as well as the other untold riches of the Indy.

5
00:03:05,930 --> 00:03:09,870
 I would like first to hear the opinion of Archbishop Fonseca, head of the Indies Council.

6
00:03:10,070 --> 00:03:14,320
 It is always interesting, Your Majesty, to know more about our distant possessions.

7
00:03:14,810 --> 00:03:19,390
 But look here, Magellan, how is that as a Portuguese you do not address your sovereign,

8
00:03:19,390 --> 00:03:21,330
 my estimable cousin Manuel I?

9
00:03:21,830 --> 00:03:23,690
 Who better could finance your project?

10
00:03:24,630 --> 00:03:27,250
 Majesty, I serve my country with honor in Africa and the Indies,

11
00:03:27,450 --> 00:03:29,390
 but a Moor's arrow in Africa struck.

12
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:32,190
 Now everybody says that I'm good for nothing.

13
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:37,590
 Hmm. What will be the direction that you will take your expedition on?

14
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:42,050
 We'll sail around South America. There's a strait there. It's right there, Your Majesty.

15
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:44,790
 Are you absolutely certain?

16
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:46,880
 I would stake my life on it, Majesty.

17
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:50,370
 I would like to see them.

18
00:03:50,780 --> 00:03:51,520
 See them?

19
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:54,020
 My glasses. Of course, here they are.

20
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:59,560
 Looking through these glass pieces, everything's fuzzy. I fail to see how you'll find the passage.

21
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:04,140
 The king is giving a twentieth of all the riches we acquire and five ships.

22
00:04:04,220 --> 00:04:06,700
 Do you realize he's giving us five ocean-going ships?

23
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:08,570
 Wait until we see them.

24
00:04:09,420 --> 00:04:11,800
 Perhaps I was wrong to dismiss Magellan.

25
00:04:11,980 --> 00:04:14,760
 If he finds a passage, it will not be so good for Portugal.

26
00:04:16,360 --> 00:04:17,120
 What is to be done?

27
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:19,500
 Just illuminate him.

28
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:20,460
 No, no, no.

29
00:04:20,540 --> 00:04:24,040
 But you, Alvarez, go off to Seville and try to stop the expedition.

30
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:25,140
 Don't let it leave.

31
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:26,580
 It won't leave, Your Majesty.

32
00:04:26,580 --> 00:04:28,560
 You may be sure of that.

33
00:04:35,430 --> 00:04:37,970
 Look, rotten timber every ship.

34
00:04:38,210 --> 00:04:40,310
 It will be a year before we leave.

35
00:04:40,310 --> 00:04:41,960
 They all have to be repaired.

36
00:05:31,350 --> 00:05:38,240
 This is Juan the captain again, your second captain. He'll command the San Antonio.

37
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:43,290
 Here is Quesada. He will command the Concepción.

38
00:05:43,290 --> 00:05:45,580
 No doubt a Spaniard.

39
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:47,330
 Yes, as it happens.

40
00:05:47,330 --> 00:05:51,720
 Well, it appears it won't be an easy voyage.

41
00:05:51,720 --> 00:05:53,210
 And what is your name?

42
00:05:53,210 --> 00:05:57,160
 My name is Albo, a pilot and helmsman. I'm Greek captain, sir.

43
00:05:57,160 --> 00:05:58,910
 A Greek. Welcome aboard, Albo.

44
00:05:58,910 --> 00:06:09,160
 Are you by chance for Captain Ferdinand Magellan, sir?

45
00:06:09,420 --> 00:06:10,720
 Yes, I am. What do you want?

46
00:06:10,820 --> 00:06:14,160
 It is my understanding that you intend to find a western route to the Indies.

47
00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:15,800
 I would like to go along.

48
00:06:15,880 --> 00:06:17,080
 And why do you want to come?

49
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:20,760
 It's going to be an extraordinary trip, and I want to note everything down.

50
00:06:21,240 --> 00:06:24,210
 This voyage is dangerous, and just where are you from?

51
00:06:24,250 --> 00:06:25,310
 Me? I'm Venetian.

52
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:28,540
 I'm Portuguese, commanding a Spanish expedition.

53
00:06:28,940 --> 00:06:32,060
 Now, Portugal and Spain are rivals in the spice trade, and Venice as well.

54
00:06:32,060 --> 00:06:36,830
 I find it very curious that someone from Venice wants to go on my voyage just for fun.

55
00:06:36,850 --> 00:06:40,270
 Naturally, it wouldn't be to discover my secret route to the east.

56
00:06:40,270 --> 00:06:46,510
 I don't represent Venice. I am Antonio Pigafetta, a Venetian gentleman, a knight of roads.

57
00:06:46,510 --> 00:06:50,490
 I just want to chronicle the story of your trip. I will pay my way if you like.

58
00:06:50,490 --> 00:06:55,120
 Very well, Antonio. But remember, nobody is certain of returning alive.

59
00:06:55,120 --> 00:06:57,930
 My servant here will show you to your quarters.

60
00:06:57,930 --> 00:07:03,940
 Hey, Fernando! I'm unable to depart on this expedition, and neither can you, I fear.

61
00:07:03,940 --> 00:07:06,000
 And why is that, my friend? Why not?

62
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:13,520
 It's my horoscope. It says that I won't survive the journey. And as for you, it's the same thing. You won't live. We'll just have to call it off.

63
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:18,100
 We can't fight our destiny. I will be terribly unhappy taking the voyage without you.

64
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:26,970
 The flotilla will follow me at all times, gentlemen. At night, a fire will burn. You are not to lose contact with my ship.

65
00:07:27,270 --> 00:07:31,030
 Two fires if I change course, three for dropping sails, understand? Those are my orders.

66
00:07:31,320 --> 00:07:33,310
 And what will our course be exactly, Captain?

67
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:37,360
 The Canary Islands. I'll give you additional orders as required. That'll be all.

68
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:39,420
 Well, we shall see.

69
00:07:39,420 --> 00:08:05,050
 Captain, aren't we headed for the Indies by sailing west?

70
00:08:05,050 --> 00:08:09,950
 West would be that direction toward the setting sun, but we're headed in a southerly direction, Captain!

71
00:08:09,950 --> 00:08:15,070
 Yes, Antonio, you're right. The ship is on a southerly course, but I wouldn't worry, we're on the right one.

72
00:08:15,070 --> 00:08:17,180
 You're sure this way we'll reach the Indies?

73
00:08:17,180 --> 00:08:19,420
 You can't be sure of anything in this life.

74
00:08:19,420 --> 00:08:28,990
 Follow Magellan's signal flame orders of our commander, until further orders, hmm.

75
00:08:28,990 --> 00:08:44,380
 First, fail!

76
00:08:44,380 --> 00:08:59,930
 We're sailing west! You very nearly put us all under water!

77
00:08:59,930 --> 00:09:05,050
 I've no need to justify my orders. I command this flotilla in the name of the King of Spain.

78
00:09:05,050 --> 00:09:07,550
 You did, but now I'm commanding Magellan.

79
00:09:07,550 --> 00:09:09,550
 Castagena, you are under arrest.

80
00:09:09,550 --> 00:09:10,910
 Put him in irons!

81
00:09:10,910 --> 00:09:15,740
 Put Magellan in irons, that's the arrangement!

82
00:09:15,740 --> 00:09:18,490
 Mutiny is punishable by death hanging!

83
00:09:18,490 --> 00:09:22,940
 Just a little mistake, Admiral, I assure you.

84
00:09:22,940 --> 00:09:26,140
 In the hold with the prisoner. Obey my order.

85
00:09:26,140 --> 00:09:29,990
 And as for you, watch your step. I won't be so lenient next time.

86
00:09:29,990 --> 00:09:32,770
 Now go back to your ships and follow until further orders.

87
00:09:32,770 --> 00:09:46,940
 Southwest, we'll be able to take on supplies at last, just in time.

88
00:09:48,440 --> 00:09:50,280
 Playing fish!

89
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:51,390
 No, but move ahead!

90
00:10:31,420 --> 00:10:35,690
 No, not all that.

91
00:10:35,690 --> 00:10:46,430
 And we're in the Southern Hemisphere. Winter starts!

92
00:10:46,430 --> 00:10:48,830
 I think that we'd better await spring here, Antonio.

93
00:10:48,830 --> 00:10:50,880
 The ships will be sheltered during the winter.

94
00:10:50,880 --> 00:10:53,440
 We're not far from the Antarctic.

95
00:10:53,440 --> 00:10:56,720
 This is a good place for us to stay and wait for warmer weather.

96
00:10:56,720 --> 00:10:57,910
 We've no choice.

97
00:10:57,910 --> 00:11:00,470
 But it means staying here for weeks, even months!

98
00:11:00,470 --> 00:11:01,510
 Yes, three or four months.

99
00:11:01,510 --> 00:11:02,430
 What the hell?!

100
00:11:02,430 --> 00:11:03,970
 Somebody wants to return to Spain!

101
00:11:03,970 --> 00:11:07,490
 Nobody believes in your impossible secret passage now!

102
00:11:07,490 --> 00:11:10,430
 I don't care, that's an order and you will obey.

103
00:11:10,430 --> 00:11:12,830
 Quesada, you watch your step.

104
00:11:12,830 --> 00:11:22,270
 We're going to mutiny, we'll take the San Antonio first and we'll get rid of that Portuguese noose, sir.

105
00:11:23,260 --> 00:11:30,930
 Mosquito, you're under arrest!

106
00:11:34,960 --> 00:11:35,740
 Stop!

107
00:11:35,740 --> 00:11:39,520
 For mutiny, you'll find out who lives in noose.

108
00:11:39,520 --> 00:11:52,440
 We're going to mutiny Spanish, he'll be with us.

109
00:11:52,440 --> 00:11:53,740
 We can count on him.

110
00:11:53,740 --> 00:11:55,640
 Ready, ready, ready.

111
00:11:55,640 --> 00:11:56,060
 Shhh.

112
00:11:56,060 --> 00:11:56,830
 We'll eat them.

113
00:11:56,830 --> 00:12:00,350
 Make a move, what about it, Mendoza, are you with us?

114
00:12:00,350 --> 00:12:02,810
 Chase, I'll get my sword, won't be a minute.

115
00:12:02,810 --> 00:12:10,400
 What's happened, Cartagena? Does it mean you've just been released?

116
00:12:10,400 --> 00:12:13,000
 Yeah, I'm taking command of our expedition, finally.

117
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:13,680
 I'm Magellan.

118
00:12:13,680 --> 00:12:14,620
 That's enough talk!

119
00:12:14,620 --> 00:12:15,940
 Oh.

120
00:12:15,940 --> 00:12:19,580
 Oh.

121
00:12:19,580 --> 00:12:22,430
 What is it, Antonio? You run as if the devils were at your heel.

122
00:12:22,430 --> 00:12:26,490
 Captain, it's a mutiny. They've liberated Cartagena and have commandeered three ships.

123
00:12:26,490 --> 00:12:27,390
 Scoggles!

124
00:12:27,390 --> 00:12:28,860
 I'll hang them!

125
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:37,050
 Sada says he wants to negotiate. Fine.

126
00:12:37,050 --> 00:12:38,650
 He may come aboard.

127
00:12:38,650 --> 00:12:42,670
 He agrees. He will speak to you aboard his ship.

128
00:12:42,670 --> 00:12:45,790
 I smell a rat. I will see him in a neutral rowboat.

129
00:12:45,790 --> 00:12:46,400
 Go on.

130
00:12:46,400 --> 00:12:51,780
 They're on their way here. When their boat draws alongside our ship, they won't be seen on the San Antonio.

131
00:12:51,780 --> 00:12:54,110
 Quickly, put 24 sailors in a rowboat. Now!

132
00:12:54,110 --> 00:13:13,920
 Sailors back on the Victoria. All right, what's Magellan's in?

133
00:13:13,920 --> 00:13:33,980
 Possession of three ships. We can block the entrance.

134
00:13:33,980 --> 00:13:58,050
 Sir, but I couldn't.

135
00:13:58,050 --> 00:14:09,530
 One less.

136
00:14:09,530 --> 00:14:11,030
 Now we've one more to recover.

137
00:14:11,030 --> 00:14:11,650
 That ship.

138
00:14:11,650 --> 00:14:21,690
 Can I can't?

139
00:14:21,690 --> 00:14:24,450
 The mutineers will be tried tomorrow.

140
00:14:24,450 --> 00:14:32,380
 Sara, you instigated a mutiny, and you killed an officer of his majesty. Do you admit to this?

141
00:14:32,380 --> 00:14:32,930
 Yeah.

142
00:14:32,930 --> 00:14:36,220
 You, Contagina, were one of the leaders in the mutiny?

143
00:14:36,220 --> 00:14:40,730
 Yes, but I surrendered, and I never killed anyone at all.

144
00:14:40,730 --> 00:14:43,420
 As for you others, you are weak men.

145
00:14:43,420 --> 00:14:45,410
 You were led into rebellion.

146
00:14:45,410 --> 00:14:49,890
 Kasada, you murdered, and you must be given your punishment, sentenced to decapitation.

147
00:14:49,890 --> 00:14:52,260
 You, Cartagena, you stood a mutiny.

148
00:14:52,260 --> 00:14:55,170
 Every navy in the world punishes mutineers with a sentence of...

149
00:14:55,170 --> 00:14:58,860
 But fortunately, you refrain from killing.

150
00:14:58,860 --> 00:15:02,240
 Therefore, your life will be spared. You will remain in this land.

151
00:15:02,240 --> 00:15:05,210
 The rest will be hanged, and may God have mercy.

152
00:15:05,210 --> 00:15:09,370
 To all of you, I grant a pardon. All of you will be needed when we sail again.

153
00:15:09,370 --> 00:15:13,050
 And you have Magellan to thank for preserving your life.

154
00:15:13,050 --> 00:15:27,420
 If it's Santiago and carry out a reconnaissance mission to the south, we will wait for you here.

155
00:15:27,420 --> 00:15:30,300
 It's two weeks now, they've been gone.

156
00:15:30,300 --> 00:15:31,640
 We can't abandon Hope.

157
00:15:31,640 --> 00:15:32,480
 What's all this?

158
00:15:32,480 --> 00:15:34,210
 They're back!

159
00:15:34,210 --> 00:15:36,220
 You've returned safe.

160
00:15:36,220 --> 00:15:39,680
 But the Santiago has been sunk, a sudden storm, and she foundered.

161
00:15:39,680 --> 00:15:43,360
 But you are alive, that's the most important thing, because you all returned.

162
00:15:43,360 --> 00:15:46,590
 All right, let's go!

163
00:15:46,590 --> 00:15:54,050
 Find bottom!

164
00:15:54,050 --> 00:15:57,120
 A splendid sign, it means it's most likely a strait.

165
00:15:57,120 --> 00:16:02,330
 I know it's right here, I'm sure.

166
00:16:02,330 --> 00:16:03,710
 Take the Conception.

167
00:16:03,710 --> 00:16:10,110
 you and the san antonio will sail on reconnaissance there's a passage we'll follow you in aye aye captain

168
00:16:10,110 --> 00:16:26,330
 passage second course west southwest that's it they've entered the passage yes i was absolutely

169
00:16:31,770 --> 00:16:39,280
 fabulous captain but you were sure of the passage right you knew it all the time my young friend

170
00:16:39,280 --> 00:16:48,930
 nothing is ever certain but i am sure of that passage forward take the southwest branch and the

171
00:16:48,930 --> 00:16:53,640
 the Victoria will follow. You, Serao, will take the southeast branch with the San Antonio.

172
00:16:55,930 --> 00:16:58,460
 Where is the San Antonio?

173
00:16:58,600 --> 00:17:03,200
 The planet Gomez put Captain Serao in irons. I believe he sailed back to Spain, but I can't...

174
00:17:03,200 --> 00:17:06,500
 That's terrible, because it was transporting almost all of our provisions.

175
00:17:07,150 --> 00:17:12,720
 Today is Wednesday, the 28th of November, 1520. We have sailed through the Straits and are

176
00:17:12,720 --> 00:17:16,520
 entering another ocean. It is very peaceful. Magellan named it the Pacific.

177
00:17:16,990 --> 00:17:21,230
 Well, Antonio, I trust you've put down everything. You should write, it's the first time ships

178
00:17:21,230 --> 00:17:24,550
 have navigated from the Atlantic Ocean into the Pacific Ocean.

179
00:17:24,790 --> 00:17:25,390
 Yes, Captain.

180
00:17:25,670 --> 00:17:29,220
 How much further do we have to sail before we reach the Indies?

181
00:17:29,460 --> 00:17:32,340
 The astronomer said 1,000 miles, but, um...

182
00:17:32,340 --> 00:17:32,700
 But?

183
00:17:32,930 --> 00:17:34,940
 But I believe it's more like thousands.

184
00:17:36,360 --> 00:17:37,260
 Magellan was right.

185
00:17:37,320 --> 00:17:39,140
 He sailed some 12,000 miles.

186
00:17:39,380 --> 00:17:41,960
 One of the most arduous voyages ever undertaken.

187
00:17:45,060 --> 00:17:49,700
 A month and a half since the passage and still no land in sight.

188
00:17:50,060 --> 00:17:52,840
 Antonio, we must have covered more than 3,000 miles.

189
00:17:52,840 --> 00:17:54,580
 The astronomer was mistaken.

190
00:17:55,010 --> 00:17:55,770
 They're all wrong.

191
00:17:56,010 --> 00:18:01,590
 Even the maps are wrong. We're running low on drinking water and food. We'll have to begin rationing everything.

192
00:18:02,530 --> 00:18:07,670
 Three months with no fresh food and almost no water, and what we do get is yellow and brackish.

193
00:18:09,430 --> 00:18:14,750
 We are surviving on old insect-infested rat-fowl biscuit crumbs.

194
00:18:15,380 --> 00:18:19,790
 Sometimes there are odd bits of oxhide that had protected the main yard arm.

195
00:18:19,790 --> 00:18:26,910
 The men are holding up as well as they can. Nobody talks about it, but we're all wondering if this is the end.

196
00:18:28,340 --> 00:18:29,900
 God rest his immortal soul.

197
00:18:31,300 --> 00:18:32,610
 Over a hundred days.

198
00:18:32,610 --> 00:18:34,700
 Oh, I know when will it ever end.

199
00:18:34,700 --> 00:18:38,290
 Magellan just missed the Marquisa Islands and Tahiti,

200
00:18:38,290 --> 00:18:41,880
 where abundant truth would have saved all of his men from scurvy.

201
00:18:41,880 --> 00:18:44,770
 At last, he reached the island of Guam,

202
00:18:44,770 --> 00:18:47,980
 in the Marianas Archipelago.

203
00:18:47,980 --> 00:18:52,480
 They're welcome.

204
00:18:52,480 --> 00:19:10,850
 Taking off with it as well!

205
00:19:10,850 --> 00:19:23,000
 This is an island of thieves!

206
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:34,680
 It's finally filled up.

207
00:19:34,680 --> 00:19:36,200
 Can't be the Moluccas.

208
00:19:36,200 --> 00:19:37,530
 We're too far north.

209
00:19:37,530 --> 00:19:39,820
 We'll stop here and rest.

210
00:19:39,820 --> 00:19:41,630
 And we'll take on provisions.

211
00:19:41,630 --> 00:19:44,760
 Come and see!

212
00:19:44,760 --> 00:19:46,720
 Oh, come and see what I found!

213
00:19:46,720 --> 00:20:01,220
 What do you want?

214
00:20:01,220 --> 00:20:04,100
 Says the chief invites us to his village.

215
00:20:04,100 --> 00:20:05,440
 Yes, with pleasure.

216
00:20:05,440 --> 00:20:06,590
 We too greet him.

217
00:20:06,590 --> 00:20:16,530
 I'm glad you understand their language.

218
00:20:16,530 --> 00:20:18,910
 Yes, it reminds me of my native tongue.

219
00:20:18,910 --> 00:20:21,630
 That's right, your home isn't very far away.

220
00:20:21,630 --> 00:20:33,330
 And that means that you're the first man ever to go around the world.

221
00:20:33,330 --> 00:20:41,880
 His nephew wanted to be baptized to show that they are friends.

222
00:20:41,880 --> 00:20:42,990
 Go tell Padre.

223
00:20:42,990 --> 00:20:47,280
 I baptize you Charles New Ferdinand in the name of...

224
00:20:47,280 --> 00:20:50,710
 I message for you, from Charles and Ferdinand.

225
00:20:50,710 --> 00:20:51,640
 Who are they?

226
00:20:51,640 --> 00:20:53,560
 You know, sir, the chief and his nephew.

227
00:20:53,560 --> 00:20:55,770
 Oh yes, and what exactly do they want?

228
00:20:55,770 --> 00:21:00,730
 They're Christians that you're their brother, and it is your duty to defeat their, uh...

229
00:21:00,730 --> 00:21:01,970
 Yes, defeat who?

230
00:21:01,970 --> 00:21:03,190
 Lapu-Lapu-Lapu.

231
00:21:03,190 --> 00:21:04,990
 No one say that we're here in peace.

232
00:21:04,990 --> 00:21:07,580
 If you don't come, Captain, they'll think you're scared.

233
00:21:07,580 --> 00:21:09,200
 All right, I'll come.

234
00:21:09,200 --> 00:21:12,800
 No, Captain, you mustn't go. It'll be much too dangerous. Your life is...

235
00:21:12,800 --> 00:21:15,010
 Confieve their conversion was to trap us.

236
00:21:15,010 --> 00:21:19,970
 If my men go into battle, then I will lead them.

237
00:21:19,970 --> 00:21:32,170
 Ask your life, Captain. We need you.

238
00:21:32,170 --> 00:21:38,460
 I won't. Don't worry, Antonio.

239
00:21:38,460 --> 00:22:00,650
 This day, the 27th of April, 1521, in the Philippines, Ferdinand Magellan,

240
00:22:01,130 --> 00:22:04,590
 after discovering the sea passage between the Atlantic and Pacific, died.

241
00:22:05,090 --> 00:22:06,670
 Our guiding light is extinguished.

242
00:22:09,660 --> 00:22:14,440
 He didn't go around the world, did he?

243
00:22:14,980 --> 00:22:17,870
 Well, he had been in India long before this voyage,

244
00:22:18,070 --> 00:22:20,030
 so Magellan did go around the world.

245
00:22:20,230 --> 00:22:25,310
 But what he demonstrated was that you can sail around South America's southern coast.

246
00:22:25,790 --> 00:22:28,910
 Did Magellan's ships ever go back? Did they return to Spain?

247
00:22:29,590 --> 00:22:33,090
 Of the five ships that had left, only one returned home.

248
00:22:33,940 --> 00:22:34,660
 Just one?

249
00:22:34,680 --> 00:22:38,400
 Yeah, that's right. And of the 218 sailors, only 18 returned.

250
00:22:38,400 --> 00:22:41,490
 We now have three ships, and only a hundred men.

251
00:22:41,490 --> 00:22:43,430
 Undermanned, the ships can't operate.

252
00:22:43,430 --> 00:22:45,720
 We'll have to abandon one of them.

253
00:22:45,720 --> 00:22:49,320
 You're right. We'll abandon the Concepcion. She's in bad condition, anyway.

254
00:22:49,320 --> 00:22:53,530
 And so they burned the Concepcion, the Trinidad, and the Victoria Sailor.

255
00:22:53,530 --> 00:22:56,700
 Finally, the Molucca Islands! They're called the Spice Islands.

256
00:22:56,700 --> 00:22:59,160
 We can just put in and see what they're like.

257
00:22:59,160 --> 00:23:06,030
 Saffron Clothes! Look at all of them!

258
00:23:06,030 --> 00:23:07,390
 Cinnamon and red pepper.

259
00:23:07,390 --> 00:23:09,020
 They're worth a fortune.

260
00:23:09,020 --> 00:23:11,770
 You like?

261
00:23:11,770 --> 00:23:34,580
 I'd rather we sail around the tip of Africa. It's much shorter.

262
00:23:35,300 --> 00:23:37,860
 Yes, it's a shorter route. However, we could run into the Portuguese.

263
00:23:38,340 --> 00:23:42,160
 He's right. I would prefer the Pacific route. With the Trinidad, it's much safer.

264
00:23:42,610 --> 00:23:44,140
 That way is much too long.

265
00:23:45,040 --> 00:23:48,240
 Well, the Victoria and the Trinidad were never to return.

266
00:23:48,580 --> 00:23:52,280
 After six months of hard sailing, the Victoria finally returned to Seville.

267
00:23:52,280 --> 00:23:55,200
 But the ship and men were in a sorry state.

268
00:23:59,040 --> 00:24:01,280
 Just think, 18 survivors.

269
00:24:02,060 --> 00:24:05,830
 And when they set out, they had been more than 200 on five ships.

270
00:24:11,210 --> 00:24:18,900
 So, all these spices are for approximately 10,000 times their cost.

271
00:24:19,080 --> 00:24:22,000
 My congratulations, you're entitled to your full shares.

272
00:24:22,780 --> 00:24:26,120
 But first, we must deduct the amount we paid for the spices,

273
00:24:26,340 --> 00:24:29,600
 your provisions, and naturally the price of the four ships that were lost.

274
00:24:29,600 --> 00:24:32,600
 And you will agree that Victoria is in terrible condition,

275
00:24:32,920 --> 00:24:36,160
 really a write-off for us, so we deduct its value from the profits.

276
00:24:37,160 --> 00:24:41,360
 Now we must deduct four-fifths of the total sum, which belongs to the king.

277
00:24:41,760 --> 00:24:44,070
 And the rest you can share between you.

278
00:24:47,370 --> 00:24:49,400
 Hello, my friends.

279
00:24:49,740 --> 00:24:53,860
 With this money, I invite you to the best dinner that any of us has had since...

280
00:24:53,860 --> 00:24:57,240
 since exactly how many days were we away in all, Antonio?

281
00:24:57,520 --> 00:24:58,960
 It was more than a thousand days.

282
00:24:59,280 --> 00:25:01,640
 Oh, the best meal we've had in one thousand days.

283
00:25:02,200 --> 00:25:03,180
 And then what?

284
00:25:03,380 --> 00:25:05,600
 Well, then I go back to sea again.

285
00:25:05,760 --> 00:25:08,820
 The sharks are much less dangerous than the ones you find on shore.

286
00:25:08,820 --> 00:25:10,480
 And what will you do, Elcano?

287
00:25:10,620 --> 00:25:12,600
 The same. I can't bear being ashore any longer.

288
00:25:12,740 --> 00:25:15,180
 But you, Antonio, you are forbidden to go on any ship.

289
00:25:15,440 --> 00:25:17,420
 You stay and write the history of our voyage.

290
00:25:17,760 --> 00:25:20,720
 The story of Magellan, who has done what nobody else had.

291
00:25:20,970 --> 00:25:22,190
 They sailed around the world.

292
00:25:22,190 --> 00:25:24,150
 It's our story, too. Don't forget to mention us.

293
00:25:24,170 --> 00:25:27,070
 It was truly an adventure. An almost unbelievable trip.

294
00:25:27,430 --> 00:25:30,050
 And without the heroism of one visionary, Ferdinand Magellan,

295
00:25:30,210 --> 00:25:31,170
 it wouldn't have been done.

296
00:25:31,430 --> 00:25:34,370
 It is unlikely that anyone will ever sail such a voyage again.

